WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:08.877
Wow.

00:00:08.877 --> 00:00:09.960
Applause at the beginning.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:10.639
Thanks, Hunter.

00:00:10.639 --> 00:00:12.180
So that person who
just wooed, I just

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.950
want to shout out,
that's Hunter Glands.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.600
He's also at Cal Poly.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.762
We're both faculty.

00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:18.720
And he helped me with
this project, is involved

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.660
in the coding, and everything.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.000
We decided not to switch off
talking in a short session.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.620
But if you have
questions, et cetera,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.870
you can ask him as well.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:29.520
So right there, making noises.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.020
OK.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:32.430
So in this talk, I
just want to tell you

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.920
about what I've done
in the really, really

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.900
intro-level classes.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:40.025
So the way I'm going to
structure this-- basically,

00:00:40.025 --> 00:00:41.399
there's a lot of
window dressing.

00:00:41.399 --> 00:00:43.650
I want to give you some
background on what courses

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.350
we have at Cal Poly, the
advantages and challenges

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.420
of using R in some of
these classes, which I'm

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.150
sure many of you
are familiar with,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.320
and then what my lab
assignments look like,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.290
what are the students
being asked to do.

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.914
And then the reason
for the hamburger--

00:00:56.914 --> 00:00:59.430
apart from it being
mid-afternoon and lunch has

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.840
worn off--

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.270
is that the meat of this
talk is these pre-lab

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.010
exercises that Hunter
and I have built

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.830
in Shiny that have kind
of helped us introduce R

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.330
to beginners.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.700
And then, I don't know,
I've got some-- is that OK?

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:14.377
Some student
outcome information.

00:01:14.377 --> 00:01:16.210
I've surveyed the
students, so hopefully you

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.176
think lettuce is also
important to hamburgers.

00:01:19.176 --> 00:01:20.800
And oh, I forgot that
I have a clicker.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.450
I'm not used to this fanciness.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:22.950
Yeah.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.900
So and then some take-home
messages, advice if any of you

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.480
are teaching such classes.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.730
So at Cal Poly, the
courses using R--

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:33.880
we do have a dedicated R class,
so that one you would hope

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.554
involves R in some way.

00:01:36.554 --> 00:01:38.470
But we also have a lot
of upper-level classes,

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.330
and those will
involve some coding

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.730
in order to get the
concepts across, et cetera.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.560
Most of the professors
seem to favor R,

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:48.567
although it's up to
our decision there.

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:50.650
But then we also have these
service-level classes,

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.410
is what we call the introductory
classes for non-majors.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.390
And these are the
classes where there's

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.680
no stat or CS background.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.929
There's no prerequisites
of any kind.

00:01:58.929 --> 00:02:00.970
And so everyone agrees
that these students should

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:03.820
see software in some
way, but typically people

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.040
are using applets.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.990
There's some debate over
whether we should use R or use

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:10.630
something-- an applet,
or jump, or Minitab--

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:14.080
some of these easier
point-and-click approaches.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:15.100
That's debatable.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:18.700
There's no opposition
to using R as the tool,

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.410
but what is really
kind of unpopular

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:22.750
is that students in these
classes should code,

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.880
because these are not
stat or CS majors.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:27.310
For my class, they're
bio majors, viticulture,

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:30.961
food science, animal
science, things like that.

00:02:30.961 --> 00:02:32.710
So this is the class
I want to talk about,

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:35.676
is the service-level
class that I teach.

00:02:35.676 --> 00:02:38.050
And I do think there's a lot
of advantages to introducing

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.940
coding in this class.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.540
You may or may
not agree with me.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.480
Pedagogically, I
think that thinking

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.880
through things with code forces
them to not just memorize

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:46.690
steps.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:49.780
What are the steps of a
hypothesis test, or a t-test?

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:50.620
Same thing.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:52.180
Linear regression, et cetera.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.180
They have to plan ahead,
plan their output,

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.090
and not memorize formulas.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:00.490
Conceptually, I think
we live in an age

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.090
where data is all digital.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.000
And so thinking of data
as a digital object,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.340
and how do people
interact with it,

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:07.840
it's a good way to
kind of interface

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.570
with how this is being
done in the real world,

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.070
and to think of
data as information,

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:16.295
and not a list of numbers
that's handed to you on an exam.

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:18.670
And then practically speaking,
of course, not all of them

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.610
are going to be data
scientists or creators.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.070
But they do need
to understand how

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.620
it's done in the real world.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:31.000
I don't know why we are still
using these t tables here

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.872
and normal z tables.

00:03:32.872 --> 00:03:34.330
Nobody uses that
in the real world.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.038
We're all just going
to use pnorm, qnorm,

00:03:36.038 --> 00:03:39.910
or our language of choice, if
this were not an R conference.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.460
It makes no sense to be using
things in class that are never,

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:45.140
ever, ever going to be
encountered in the real world.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.690
So this is my opinion,
anyway, that I

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:50.410
think these students should
benefit from seeing code.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.790
It's not always easy, though.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:53.540
I keep forgetting.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:54.850
You know, we don't
have these at Cal Poly.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:55.947
It's a state school.

00:03:55.947 --> 00:03:58.507
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:58.507 --> 00:04:00.340
Some of the challenges
that I've encountered

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.800
with these new students--

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.560
logistically, I went
in the first time

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:06.730
I used R in this class.

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:07.360
I was gung ho.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.480
I had put so much thought
into how to make this gentle,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.220
and introduce them to coding.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.462
And what I instead dealt
with for the whole class is,

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:15.670
where is my downloads folder?

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:18.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:19.579
These kids are 17 to 23.

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:22.120
I thought they'd grown up with
computers, and it was natural.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.620
But I've learned there's
kind of a sweet spot

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:25.390
in between the people that
are frightened of computers,

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:26.806
and the people
that are frightened

00:04:26.806 --> 00:04:28.570
of downloads folders.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.642
So that was a challenge.

00:04:31.642 --> 00:04:33.100
There's ways around
it, and I don't

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:34.120
want to dwell on this too much.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.380
This is very important
to the class,

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:36.879
but it's not really
my contribution,

00:04:36.879 --> 00:04:39.790
so there's many better people
than I to speak of this.

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.250
But I just want to mention,
using cloud servers has really

00:04:42.250 --> 00:04:43.520
helped in this class.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.160
RStudio Cloud is awesome.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.690
Amazon Web Services--
you can set up

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.930
your own home cloud, which is
what we've done at Cal Poly.

00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:53.050
And then using notebooks
to feed them code--

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:56.110
that helps-- or to call
your own scripts, et cetera.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:57.460
GitHub Classroom is really cool.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:58.918
I've just gotten
started with that.

00:04:58.918 --> 00:05:01.090
That's a nice way to just
package everything off

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:02.250
to these students.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.800
And then they use this package
that's kind of idiot-proof,

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:07.240
as they say.

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.470
So these things can help.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.178
I would just point
you to those if you're

00:05:10.178 --> 00:05:12.910
interested in using R for
people that are frightened

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.510
of downloads folders.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:17.470
But anyway, the other
challenges that I

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:22.150
run into, besides those
logistics, are pedagogical.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:24.100
I want to involve
coding in my teaching,

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.270
but I'm not teaching coding.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:26.978
I'm teaching statistics,
and I'm teaching

00:05:26.978 --> 00:05:28.030
concepts of statistics.

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:31.600
And so how do I do that without
ending up just teaching coding?

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.090
And what I get in
feedback a lot is,

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.821
we spent all this time learning
R, and it wasn't on the exam.

00:05:36.821 --> 00:05:39.070
I did put R output that they
have to read on the exam,

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:40.486
but they don't
have to write code,

00:05:40.486 --> 00:05:43.100
there's no coding
questions, and so forth.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:47.890
So this is a big question, of
how to synthesize those ideas.

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.360
And then these
students are mostly

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.943
students who are forced to take
stat, don't want to take stat,

00:05:51.943 --> 00:05:53.479
they don't think they like math.

00:05:53.479 --> 00:05:55.270
And so it just adds
one more thing for them

00:05:55.270 --> 00:05:59.470
to be scared about
in this intro class.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:00.834
So I did a survey.

00:06:00.834 --> 00:06:02.500
I did some extensive
surveys of my class

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.010
this fall that ended
just in December.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:07.755
And this is what the
students had to say.

00:06:07.755 --> 00:06:09.880
I had the question, I feel
nervous about my ability

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:11.530
to complete the lab assignment.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.662
And over the first three
lab assignments here,

00:06:13.662 --> 00:06:14.620
they're pretty nervous.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:15.995
More than half of
them are saying

00:06:15.995 --> 00:06:17.530
that they strongly
agree or agree

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:18.850
that they're nervous about it.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.110
And it wasn't really
getting better, actually,

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:21.040
across the assignments.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.640
So this is something
they find daunting,

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.223
and I will readily admit that,
that it's a challenge for them.

00:06:27.782 --> 00:06:29.490
So let me tell you
what these assignments

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:33.037
that are so terrifying
that I'm using in my class.

00:06:33.037 --> 00:06:34.120
When I first started out--

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:36.300
OK, so here's an example
of a data set that I use.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.716
I'm sure many of
you have seen it.

00:06:37.716 --> 00:06:38.940
It's the Wine dataset.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.335
We've got a whole bunch of
chemical measures of wine,

00:06:41.335 --> 00:06:43.710
and then we've got this measure
at the bottom-- quality--

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.751
that's going to say if
it's a good or a bad wine,

00:06:45.751 --> 00:06:47.610
as rated by experts.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.730
So the idea is, what
different chemical properties

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:54.084
does the wine have
to be a good wine?

00:06:54.084 --> 00:06:56.250
And so in my first version
of these labs-- and these

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:57.583
are just snippets from the labs.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:07:00.450
They're obviously much longer
and formatted differently.

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:04.314
But I just give them
this t-test code,

00:07:04.314 --> 00:07:05.730
and I ask them
questions about it.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:07.620
And their job is to
look at that output

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:09.850
and interpret it correctly.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:11.570
So it's not really coding.

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:14.830
It's reading output,
but it's not coding.

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.560
And then the next version
was, reproduce my process.

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:20.280
So first I would do the same
thing-- ask them something,

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:21.180
give them that code.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:22.680
And then I'd say--
well, here what I

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.150
asked them about was pH level.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.766
Does the pH level of the
wine matter to the quality?

00:07:26.766 --> 00:07:28.140
And then I said,
now suppose I've

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:29.820
made some claim about density.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.110
Can you back that claim up?

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:33.660
And all they're going to do is
copy, paste, and write density

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:34.160
where pH is.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.951
Or ideally that's what
they're going to do.

00:07:35.951 --> 00:07:38.790
You'd be surprised the number of
different things they would do,

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:41.040
such as writing t.test.density--

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:45.610
weird stuff like that, because
they're so new to this.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:46.950
I have a clicker.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:49.740
Version 3 is where I really
think my students are

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:50.640
being statisticians.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.700
This is where I have not
given them almost anything.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:55.590
I have said, choose
three chemical attributes

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:56.520
of the wine.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:57.600
Make me some plots.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:58.839
Do some t-tests.

00:07:58.839 --> 00:08:00.630
This is what their lab
assignment literally

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.120
looked like on this, and this
is the third lab assignment.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.050
So this is week four.

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:08.222
I don't do one the first
week of a 10-week course.

00:08:08.222 --> 00:08:09.180
So this is cool, right?

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.730
I think it's really cool that
non-majors can handle this,

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:12.990
and they really can.

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:15.870
But how do I get them
there, is the question,

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.450
without having given
them anything right here

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:19.920
to work with.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:24.600
So what we came up with, this
intermediate transition tool,

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:29.000
is these pre-lab
exercises in Shiny.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.580
And so where this
idea came from was

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.289
at ICOTS, the conference on
teaching statistics in Japan

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:35.870
last summer, Hunter and
I went to two talks.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:37.370
I think there's a
rule that you have

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:39.289
to quote Hadley if you're going
to talk at this conference.

00:08:39.289 --> 00:08:40.284
That's what I heard.

00:08:40.284 --> 00:08:42.409
But really, there were
these two talks back to back

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:43.492
that really influenced us.

00:08:43.492 --> 00:08:46.010
The main one was
Amelia McNamara saying,

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:48.440
"We need a bridge between"--
there's learning statistics.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:49.100
Those are applets.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.308
And there's doing statistics.

00:08:50.308 --> 00:08:53.559
That's being a true
programmer or coder.

00:08:53.559 --> 00:08:55.850
And there's not really a
bridge in between these things

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:56.606
right now.

00:08:56.606 --> 00:08:57.980
And this kind of
matched a vision

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.970
that I've had for a long
time floating in my brain.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:03.500
And then Hadley backing up
that not all statisticians need

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:06.500
to be coders, but if you're
not interacting with code,

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:07.980
are you really doing statistics?

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.570
I think the challenges
and the advantages

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:12.950
pedagogically-- they all pale
in comparison to the fact

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:15.310
that, if we're offering
to teach statistics

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:18.200
and we don't teach them to think
about it as a digital process,

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:19.730
we are not teaching
them statistics.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:20.870
That's my opinion.

00:09:20.870 --> 00:09:23.660
I'm sure many will disagree, but
I would love to argue with you.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:26.045
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:26.045 --> 00:09:28.170
So that's the idea, so
that's where this came from.

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:29.200
So Hunter and I--

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:30.920
we went upstairs
at this conference,

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:33.544
and we sat down amongst all the
Japanese business people having

00:09:33.544 --> 00:09:35.180
lunch, and we coded
for a couple hours,

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:36.930
and then spent the
whole summer continuing

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.660
to develop these things.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:42.590
So the idea is the principles
behind these tools.

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:45.982
We figured they should
have immediate results.

00:09:45.982 --> 00:09:47.690
What's nice about
applets is the students

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:50.390
go very quickly to
seeing output that

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.410
is exciting and interpretable.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:54.890
So we wanted to maintain
that, to kind of immediately

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.704
see the process.

00:09:57.704 --> 00:09:59.120
But then we wanted
to require them

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.800
to be generating
the queries, to not

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.620
be selecting from a drop-down
menu or clicking a checkbox

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:07.346
or so forth, as a
real coder would.

00:10:07.346 --> 00:10:09.620
And we also wanted to make
really clear what's built

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:11.120
into R versus what
they're choosing,

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:13.250
because this is
not an R-specific--

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:14.751
I'm not trying to
teach them R. So I

00:10:14.751 --> 00:10:16.375
want them to be able
to interact, then,

00:10:16.375 --> 00:10:18.020
with another language
and understand,

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:20.116
we have to think about
what variables we're using,

00:10:20.116 --> 00:10:21.740
even if the function
to make a box plot

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:24.722
or whatever has
a different name.

00:10:24.722 --> 00:10:27.180
And then I really wanted to
link the concepts and questions

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:27.930
from lecture--

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:32.580
the big ideas-- to what was
happening with the code.

00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.550
So I want to do a demo here.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:38.250
And I've got a
backup plan, but I

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.980
think these are working
fine on shinyapps.io.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:42.480
So I'll have these
links at the end.

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:44.640
I've got three of these
hosted on shinyapps.io

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.200
if you want to demo them.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:47.940
For the class, we
hosted them separately

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:49.920
because shinyapps
would get pummeled

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.650
and I would not be able to
handle it on my free account.

00:10:52.650 --> 00:10:55.560
But these ones are here, if
you guys want to try them.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:58.260
So the idea here is this was
their first lab exercise.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:01.410
It's built on learnr, so someone
asked a question earlier--

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:03.750
a really nice package
for this kind of thing.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:08.940
And the key here is that
what we're going to do

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:12.780
is, we have the Titanic data
set for all of these exercises,

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:14.730
and they have to
choose the variables.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.699
So let's say we want
to look at gender,

00:11:16.699 --> 00:11:18.240
and then we want to
see who survived.

00:11:21.197 --> 00:11:22.390
There we go.

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:24.820
And so what this does,
and kind of the key point,

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:27.460
is it fills in that
code line there.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:28.090
Is that OK?

00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:30.220
I can zoom in a
little bit there.

00:11:30.220 --> 00:11:32.540
So that line of code--

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:34.420
they're seeing the
blue, is the thing

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:36.375
that is a name that was chosen.

00:11:36.375 --> 00:11:37.000
But I chose it.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.559
They didn't input it just now.

00:11:38.559 --> 00:11:40.600
And the red is the stuff
that they just selected,

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:42.141
and the rest of it
is just the syntax

00:11:42.141 --> 00:11:45.460
of this particular
coding language.

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:48.190
And so then we see, OK,
they used facet_grid.

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:50.080
And you can kind of
get an idea what's

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:51.670
going on, but not so great.

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:55.000
So we can go down to stacked
bar charts, things like that,

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:56.020
make different choices.

00:11:56.020 --> 00:11:57.370
They can see the
output immediately,

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:59.710
and decide which of those plots
answers their question better.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.200
Which one is going to show
you that men tended to die,

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:05.910
and women tended to
survive on the Titanic?

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:10.740
And so additionally, we
have this idea of tying it

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.600
to the concepts in class.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:14.310
So here's the
exercise for t-tests

00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:15.780
where we can say, all right.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:19.980
I'd like to make claims
about the age on Titanic--

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:21.370
the age of the passengers.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.250
And under the
null, let's say 30.

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:26.190
And let's say we're
interested in testing

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:30.090
whether the average age was
less than 30 for some reason.

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:32.520
And so we can see this
in words that they've

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:33.420
been seeing in class.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:34.950
The hypothesis is
that the true mean

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:37.240
is equal to 30,
versus less than 30.

00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.780
We can see this in code,
again with the blue and red.

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:42.370
And then we can see the output
that they have to interpret.

00:12:42.370 --> 00:12:43.240
And learnr is nice.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:46.140
It's got a good interface
for including some questions.

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:48.990
We can say, oh, look, what
sample mean did I observe?

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:53.280
Well that was 29.557.

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:53.780
There we go.

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:56.280
And it'll tell them right away
they got it right, so they're

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:58.934
able to check and make sure that
they've gotten the output that

00:12:58.934 --> 00:12:59.475
was expected.

00:13:02.902 --> 00:13:04.610
And then the last one
I want to show you,

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:06.710
just to tie all these
principles together,

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:10.740
is the normal random
variables in general.

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:12.470
So we could say
something like, let's say

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:15.470
we have a normal random
variable with a mean of 67

00:13:15.470 --> 00:13:18.470
and a standard deviation of 3,
and I'm interested in the area

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:19.892
below 60.

00:13:19.892 --> 00:13:21.350
So again, I'm tying
this to the way

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:22.766
they've seen it
in class, which is

00:13:22.766 --> 00:13:25.160
this notation of
normally distributed

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:26.880
and what's the probability.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:28.752
And then we can see
the code, the result,

00:13:28.752 --> 00:13:30.710
and then I made these
little illustrations just

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:32.251
so they can see
what's going on, what

00:13:32.251 --> 00:13:33.500
the picture looks like there.

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:35.791
So all these things that they
see typically in lecture,

00:13:35.791 --> 00:13:38.510
all in one place, and being
dynamically generated thanks

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:42.620
to the magic of Shiny, and the
coding that Hunter and I have

00:13:42.620 --> 00:13:45.140
done, I suppose.

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:46.310
So those are the exercises.

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:50.110
There we go.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:52.331
And so let me tell you what
happened when I finally

00:13:52.331 --> 00:13:52.830
used these.

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:54.538
So I started these
out on my summer class

00:13:54.538 --> 00:13:56.390
with only 18 students,
and used them

00:13:56.390 --> 00:14:00.240
with 108 students this fall.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:02.240
The first thing is students
definitely recognize

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:03.950
that R is applicable.

00:14:03.950 --> 00:14:06.170
This question says, the
in-class lab activity

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:08.570
helped me understand how
statistical analyses are

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:09.980
conducted in the real world.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:12.241
They overwhelmingly agree,
or at least feel neutrally

00:14:12.241 --> 00:14:12.740
about that.

00:14:12.740 --> 00:14:16.490
No one is arguing with that.

00:14:16.490 --> 00:14:18.950
They recognize the
value to themselves.

00:14:18.950 --> 00:14:20.930
This one says, the
in-class activities

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:24.020
help me understand the concepts
from the week's material.

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:26.299
And you can see they,
over time, started to see

00:14:26.299 --> 00:14:27.590
more how this was helping them.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:29.173
I don't know if that
was a progression

00:14:29.173 --> 00:14:31.910
as they got more comfortable, or
if maybe that third lab, which

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:35.090
had to do with the t-tests,
was particularly helpful.

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.602
One way or the other, I
like that that was positive,

00:14:37.602 --> 00:14:39.560
and also that it improved
over these three labs

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:43.132
that I surveyed heavily on.

00:14:43.132 --> 00:14:44.090
I really like this one.

00:14:44.090 --> 00:14:45.770
I asked, I feel proud
of the work I produced

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:47.145
on the assignment,
the assignment

00:14:47.145 --> 00:14:50.030
being the actual data
analysis, not these exercises.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:51.800
And they feel very proud of it.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:54.387
Almost none of them
feel un-proud of it.

00:14:54.387 --> 00:14:55.970
And most of them
were kind of excited.

00:14:55.970 --> 00:14:57.595
And it was fun, during
these exercises,

00:14:57.595 --> 00:15:00.650
to watch them hit knit on
their file and go, oh my gosh.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:01.220
It worked.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:03.590
This is cool, and
explore this data.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:06.890
That was very rewarding
for me, anyway.

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:10.610
I surveyed them pretty heavily
on the skills they gained.

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:12.880
This one, they were asked
to agree or disagree

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:14.880
or whatever with, I feel
confident in my ability

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:16.809
to read read and
interpret a bar plot.

00:15:16.809 --> 00:15:18.600
And that one, it looks
like the lab helped.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:22.195
So I surveyed them before
anything, after the exercise,

00:15:22.195 --> 00:15:24.570
and then after the assignment
itself-- the full analysis.

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.410
And there seems to be some
improvement happening.

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:29.810
I was hoping to find some
improvement over concepts,

00:15:29.810 --> 00:15:32.150
and I didn't actually
really find that.

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:35.630
Mostly anything that was about
identifying aspects of data,

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:38.240
or describing
aspects of data, they

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:39.780
did not self-report improving.

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:42.904
Although anecdotally, I do
actually feel that they did.

00:15:42.904 --> 00:15:44.570
And then small
improvements for anything

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:47.490
to do with creating
or analyzing plots,

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:51.270
and a little bit to do
with calculating things.

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:52.740
So some feedback.

00:15:52.740 --> 00:15:54.660
I'm hanging myself out
to dry here reading

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:56.460
one of my least
favorite sentences

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:57.540
that I've ever received.

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.610
But in summer,
one of my students

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:02.970
described these as a,
"complete, unorganized,

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.250
catastrophic mess."

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.920
I was having a lot of trouble
figuring out how to host them.

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:09.930
shinyapps couldn't
handle my students,

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.960
and I had to relearn Unix
to host them elsewhere.

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:14.850
So there was some
technical difficulties.

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:17.308
I tried it on that class because
there was only 18 of them,

00:16:17.308 --> 00:16:18.510
so I could help them out.

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:21.210
But for the 108 in this past
fall, it went very smoothly.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.052
And here's three
of the comments.

00:16:23.052 --> 00:16:24.510
People are saying
that they thought

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:25.719
these labs were helpful.

00:16:25.719 --> 00:16:27.510
There were some slightly
negative comments,

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:28.440
but nothing like this one.

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:30.148
They were more, I
don't understand why we

00:16:30.148 --> 00:16:32.830
did it, instead of, I hated it.

00:16:32.830 --> 00:16:35.820
So that was encouraging.

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.880
And lastly, I didn't survey
heavily my previous classes.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:42.450
I didn't know I was going to
be experimenting like this.

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:45.000
But in winter 2018, I
also had three sections

00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:47.472
about the same time,
same number of students.

00:16:47.472 --> 00:16:48.930
Homework was pretty
much identical.

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.822
I didn't tweak it too much
between winter and fall.

00:16:51.822 --> 00:16:53.280
And the lab material
was the same--

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:54.700
the same data sets
and questions--

00:16:54.700 --> 00:16:57.660
but it was split for the fall
into this pre-lab and lab

00:16:57.660 --> 00:16:59.780
format.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:01.382
And so I asked
them two questions.

00:17:01.382 --> 00:17:03.090
Halfway through the
quarter, I asked them

00:17:03.090 --> 00:17:04.410
how much the homework
assignments helped,

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:06.270
and how much the lab
assignments helped.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:08.430
And what we found
was not a huge change

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:11.550
in the homework from winter
to fall, which you'd expect.

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:12.810
It's the same homework.

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:15.040
But the lab assignments,
if you see here--

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:16.829
oh, I keep forgetting
this exists.

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:21.380
These lab assignments
here in the winter,

00:17:21.380 --> 00:17:23.940
like 3/4 of my class
hated them, or didn't

00:17:23.940 --> 00:17:24.960
think they were useful.

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:26.412
And in the fall,
most of the class

00:17:26.412 --> 00:17:28.620
did think they were useful,
or at least felt neutral.

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:30.210
So I think that is
maybe attributable

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.080
to these exercises.

00:17:33.080 --> 00:17:35.277
So just some take-home
messages, what worked well.

00:17:35.277 --> 00:17:37.110
I liked that the students
could analyze data

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.830
from scratch themselves.

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:42.330
Anecdotally, I really think the
students ended up better off

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:44.880
in the winter class
or the fall class.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:47.640
I like seeing them get
excited about results.

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:48.450
What didn't work?

00:17:48.450 --> 00:17:50.940
Well, first of all,
these exercises--

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:52.000
we banged them out.

00:17:52.000 --> 00:17:52.920
We are not developers.

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:53.650
We're trying to be.

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:54.510
We're getting there.

00:17:54.510 --> 00:17:55.780
But we're not.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:57.750
I'm going to hang
myself out to dry again.

00:17:57.750 --> 00:17:59.190
This is what it
looks like when I

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:01.950
have to output both
the strings of code,

00:18:01.950 --> 00:18:04.440
and also evaluate that code.

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:07.892
In particular, I'm just going to
really hang myself out to dry.

00:18:07.892 --> 00:18:09.600
A lot of people I know
will get mad at me

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:11.560
for that-- eval(parse).

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:12.060
Ugh.

00:18:12.060 --> 00:18:14.080
I know it's a crime, but I
don't know what else to do.

00:18:14.080 --> 00:18:15.390
And I wanted to do
something, and something

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:16.170
is better than nothing.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:16.836
So there we are.

00:18:16.836 --> 00:18:18.055
Sorry, Dave Robinson.

00:18:18.055 --> 00:18:20.910
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:20.910 --> 00:18:22.799
And so apart from
that-- and this

00:18:22.799 --> 00:18:24.090
is why I'm sharing it with you.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:25.714
One reason is in the
hopes that there's

00:18:25.714 --> 00:18:27.660
people out there who
might want to help

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:32.940
me make this more of a usable,
sharable, manageable tool.

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:35.520
I think this idea of thinking
through code-- students tended

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:38.280
to type their variable
names for the new data

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:40.380
into the exercise, which
of course wouldn't run.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:41.921
But then they would
copy paste, which

00:18:41.921 --> 00:18:44.730
isn't quite what I was
hoping, but maybe it's OK.

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:46.620
And motivating the
students-- they really

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:48.120
were disturbed by
the fact that they

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:49.661
were spending class
time on something

00:18:49.661 --> 00:18:51.510
that wasn't on the exam.

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:52.779
So my advice for you.

00:18:52.779 --> 00:18:54.070
The growing pains are worth it.

00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:56.520
That is a headache,
but it's worth it.

00:18:56.520 --> 00:18:59.160
I think if you have an idea
for a teaching tool, build it.

00:18:59.160 --> 00:18:59.920
It's not perfect.

00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:03.300
Mine is ugly, but it's
cool and it helped.

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:06.720
And that these kids can
handle R. They really can.

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.270
They grew up with computers.

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:10.020
They can do more than
they think they can.

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:11.910
They can think about data.

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.130
They can do it, and so can
the people teaching them.

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:17.340
So I hope that more
interests classes will really

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.830
engage with coding instead of
just applets in the future.

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:21.761
That's my goal.

00:19:21.761 --> 00:19:22.260
All right.

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:25.160
So the GitHub repository
there has all the material

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:29.030
for these exercises, if you to
use, or better yet, edit them.

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:31.850
Those three demos-- the
links are here, and then

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:33.710
contact info for me or Hunter.

00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:35.831
And that's all I got.

00:19:35.831 --> 00:19:39.599
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:44.290
We have time for one question.

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:53.155
Hi.

00:19:53.155 --> 00:19:54.030
This is a great talk.

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:55.650
Thank you very much.

00:19:55.650 --> 00:19:58.320
So a lot of times when
I hear these discussions

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:01.200
about computing and
statistics pedagogy,

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:02.681
they're along two veins.

00:20:02.681 --> 00:20:04.680
One is, I think, kind of
what you're doing here,

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:07.320
which is using R and Shiny
in these kinds of tools

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:11.220
to improve the teaching
of classical topics.

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:15.270
And the other one is
to replace the teaching

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:18.570
of classical topics with more
computationally-based stuff,

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:21.300
like permutation testing,
bootstrap, stuff like that

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:24.342
right up front instead of
normal derivations of t-test,

00:20:24.342 --> 00:20:25.050
things like that.

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:27.460
I was wondering if that
second component of it

00:20:27.460 --> 00:20:29.400
is something you
and your colleagues

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:32.400
have discussed as
you're incorporating

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:35.037
more of this stuff into
the these earlier classes?

00:20:35.037 --> 00:20:35.620
Yeah, totally.

00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:38.280
So I think the question is
about using computing just

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:39.750
to reinforce things
like t-tests,

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.640
versus to introduce things
like permutation tests that

00:20:42.640 --> 00:20:45.390
get the spirit of hypothesis
testing without the math

00:20:45.390 --> 00:20:46.781
maybe necessary.

00:20:46.781 --> 00:20:48.030
Yeah, that's a big discussion.

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:50.280
I think Beth Chance
teaches a class

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:52.580
fully on simulation
permutation tests and none

00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:53.389
of the other stuff.

00:20:53.389 --> 00:20:54.930
For me, I kind of
believe in teaching

00:20:54.930 --> 00:20:57.330
the classical concepts, because
they are going to see that.

00:20:57.330 --> 00:20:58.788
Even if they never
do that, they're

00:20:58.788 --> 00:21:00.690
going to read papers
in their fields,

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:02.439
and it's probably going
to have a t-score.

00:21:02.439 --> 00:21:03.819
They should know what that is.

00:21:03.819 --> 00:21:06.360
And so mostly I've been using
it to reinforce those concepts,

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:10.069
but also I do feel
really strongly about

00:21:10.069 --> 00:21:12.360
don't do something in a class
that is never, ever going

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:14.250
to happen in the real world.

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:16.020
So as maybe those
things go away,

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:19.580
then it would make more sense
to emphasize the other stuff.

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:21.130
Yeah.

